<%- include('partials/head', {
  fullTitle: 'West Michigan Junk Removal & Cleanout Service Area | RYDJA',
  canonicalPath: '/service-area',
  // Plain text only: this lands in a meta content attribute, where an HTML
  // entity would be escaped and shown to the reader as its own source.
  description: 'Where RYDJA works: ' + serviceArea + '. Junk removal, cleanouts, hauling and yard cleanup. Just outside the area? Ask and we will answer honestly.'
}) %>

<main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="section shell narrow legal service-page">
  <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
    <a href="/services">Services</a> <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
    <span aria-current="page">Service Area</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Where RYDJA works</h1>
  <p class="lede smaller">We serve <%= serviceArea %>.</p>

  <p>
    RYDJA is a local operation, not a franchise with a call centre. That is the whole reason the service area is
    drawn the way it is: a truck, a trailer and a crew have to actually get to you, do the work, and get a load to a
    disposal site in a sensible day.
  </p>

  <%# The communities themselves come from configuration -- the same list that
      feeds areaServed in the structured data -- so the page and the markup
      cannot disagree, and nobody has to remember to update two places. There
      are deliberately no per-city pages behind these: a page per town with the
      same copy and the name swapped is a doorway page, and it would read like
      one to a visitor as much as to a crawler. %>
  <h2>Communities we serve</h2>
  <ul class="area-list">
    <% serviceAreaPlaces.forEach(function(place) { %><li><%= place %></li><% }) %>
  </ul>
  <p class="price-note">
    These are the places we have actually committed to, not a radius drawn to look impressive. If your town is not
    named, read on &mdash; the boundary is softer than a list makes it look.
  </p>

  <h2>How far we travel, and why it depends on the job</h2>
  <p>
    The honest version is that distance is priced against the size of the load, not against a map.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Smaller jobs need to make sense for the travel.</strong> A single
      <a class="text-link" href="/furniture-appliance-removal">couch or appliance pickup</a> an hour each way costs
      more in driving than in work, which is why the tighter end of the area exists at all. Close in, that job is
      easy; far out, it may not be worth either of us doing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Larger jobs justify a longer drive.</strong> An
      <a class="text-link" href="/estate-cleanouts">estate or whole-property cleanout</a> &mdash; a house, a garage,
      a basement and a barn, over however many loads it takes &mdash; is usually worth the trip to anywhere we work
      and often to somewhere past it. The travel is a small share of a job that size. If you are settling an estate,
      clearing an inherited home or getting a property empty before a sale, send the address with your photographs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Several jobs in one place beat one job far away.</strong> If you are a
      <a class="text-link" href="/property-professionals">property manager or investor</a> with two units to turn
      over in the same town, that changes the arithmetic even if the town is at the edge of the list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Disposal access matters as much as mileage.</strong> Where the load can be taken afterwards is part of
      the drive, and it is the part nobody thinks about when they measure the distance to their own house.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Just outside the list?</h2>
  <p>
    Ask anyway. The boundary is about driving time and disposal access rather than a line on a map, so a job a little
    further out can still make sense &mdash; particularly a larger one. Send it through the
    <a class="text-link" href="/quote">quote form</a> with the address and photos and we will tell you plainly whether
    we can reach it. If we cannot, we will say so rather than string you along.
  </p>

  <figure class="area-figure">
    <%# Brand photography of the RYDJA truck and trailer. Deliberately NOT
        captioned as a particular customer's job -- it is not one. Real job
        photography goes in a Recent Work section when there is some. %>
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/img/rydja-truck-trailer.webp?v=<%= assetV.img %>" type="image/webp">
      <img src="/img/rydja-truck-trailer.jpg?v=<%= assetV.img %>"
           width="1000" height="563" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="The RYDJA pickup and trailer loaded for a haul">
    </picture>
    <figcaption>The truck and trailer that turn up when you book a job.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What we do across the area</h2>
  <p>Every service is available everywhere we work:</p>
  <ul>
    <% servicePages.forEach(function(p) { %>
      <li><a class="text-link" href="/<%= p.slug %>"><%= p.card.title %></a> &mdash; <%= p.card.blurb %></li>
    <% }) %>
    <li>
      <a class="text-link" href="/property-professionals">For property professionals</a> &mdash; how Realtors,
      property managers, estate professionals and investors place cleanout work with RYDJA.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How a job runs, wherever you are</h2>
  <ol class="area-steps">
    <li>Send photos and a short description through the quote form.</li>
    <li>We review access, volume, labour and disposal, and send you a price.</li>
    <li>You approve the quote, and confirm a time we propose or ask for a different one.</li>
    <li>We turn up, clear it, and haul it off.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="price-note">
    <%= businessHours.label %>, and the quote form is open whenever you are.
  </p>

  <p class="cta-inline">
    <a class="button" href="/quote">Send photos for a quote</a>
    <% if (businessPhone) { %>
      <span class="or-call">or call <%- include('partials/call-text') %></span>
    <% } %>
  </p>
</main>

<script type="application/ld+json"><%- breadcrumbJsonLd %></script>

<%- include('partials/foot') %>
